module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.GeneratedIdealAdjoint
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.PushforwardCommutesCompletionSpaces
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-!
# `Rf_*` and derived completion at ideal sheaves generated by global sections

Let `f : (Sh(𝒟), 𝒪') ⟶ (Sh(𝒞), 𝒪)` be a morphism of ringed sites, let `t_1, …, t_s` be global
sections of `𝒪` and `g_1, …, g_r` global sections of `𝒪'`, and let `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s) ⊂ 𝒪` and
`ℐ' = (g_1, …, g_r) ⊂ 𝒪'` be the sheaves of ideals they generate. This file reads the four
statements of `PushforwardCommutesCompletion.lean` about [Stacks, Tag 0A0G] and
[Stacks, Tag 0A0I], and the three of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionSpaces.lean`, at that pair of
ideal sheaves.

Each of those seven statements carries the conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 099F] — the existence of
the derived completion functor, which is what lets `K^∧` be written at all — as an instance
hypothesis. At a sheaf of ideals generated by finitely many global sections that conclusion is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf` of
`GeneratedIdealAdjoint.lean`, which needs only the ambient ringed site and the generators. It is
declared a local instance here, so every statement below has the derived completion functors
produced from its data and no `IsRightAdjoint` argument of any kind.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionPushforwardComparisonGeneratedIdealSheaf`: the
  canonical natural map `(Rf_*K)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)` of [Stacks, Tag 0A0G] at the two generated
  ideal sheaves.

## Main results

In the namespace `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite`:

* `isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf`:
  `Rf_*(K^∧)` is derived complete along `ℐ`, for an arbitrary `K` — the left hand side of the
  display of [Stacks, Tag 0A0I].
* `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isIso_map_unit`:
  the comparison map is invertible as soon as `Rf_*(K ⟶ K^∧)` is.
* `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete`:
  the display `Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧` at a derived-complete `K`.
* `Stacks.RingedSpace.isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf`,
  `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete` in
  the namespace `Stacks.RingedSpace`, and
  `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete` in
  the namespace `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme`:
  the same three statements at a morphism of ringed spaces and at a morphism of schemes, which
  is the display's own setting. The last of these is also an instance of **[Stacks, Tag 0AKL]**:
  its object ranges over the derived-complete objects of the derived category, so it is the
  analogue of the display for complexes of quasi-coherent modules that the remark's fifth
  sentence attributes to [HL-P, Theorem 6.5]. The module documentation of
  `PushforwardCommutesCompletionSpaces.lean` goes through Remark 52.7.4 sentence by sentence.

## Implementation notes

*Hypotheses.* Everything below is proved under hypotheses on the *data* only: the two sheaves of
ideals are generated by finitely many global sections, and `f^♯` carries sections of `ℐ` into
`ℐ'`, which is the source's own relation between the two ideals. No conclusion of the source,
and no conclusion of a tag the source's proof invokes, is a hypothesis anywhere.

*The data.* A finite-type sheaf of ideals is only **locally** generated by finitely many
sections; `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s)` asks for generators on the site itself, so what is proved here is
the globally generated case of each tag. The case of a sheaf of ideals of finite type is
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`, where [Stacks, Tag 099F] is the instance
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` of `FiniteTypeReflector.lean`,
proved from the one *global* object `K ⟶ 𝒪` of [Stacks, Tag 099E] with
`E^∧ = R𝓗om_𝒪(K, E)`, namely `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isGlobalExtendedCech` of
`FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean`. The statements here are the special case exhibited by
`Stacks.RingedSpace.isFiniteType_generatedIdealSheaf` of that file: on the small Zariski site,
global generators are local generators over the trivial covering of `⊤`.

*The two generating families.* The invertibility of the comparison map for an **arbitrary** `K`
is the whole assertion of [Stacks, Tag 0A0G]; at two *corresponding* families,
`g_i = f^♯(t_i)`, which is the relation the source's own `ℐ'` satisfies, it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`. The statements here keep the two families
independent, related only by `PushforwardIdealCompatible`, which is strictly more general as a
statement about two sheaves of ideals and strictly less than the source's relation; at that
generality the object is restricted instead, by `isIso_…_of_isDerivedComplete` to the
derived-complete objects, where the display is near-trivial, or by `isIso_…_of_isIso_map_unit` to
those with `Rf_*K` already derived complete.

*Why a separate module.* `PushforwardCommutesCompletion.lean` sits below
`GeneratedIdealAdjoint.lean` in the import order — the latter needs the whole Čech development,
including the internal-hom vanishing criteria, and the former needs none of it — so these
specializations cannot live beside the general statements without pulling that development into
every consumer of them.

*The two index sets.* `ℐ` and `ℐ'` are generated by families of different lengths, indexed
independently. The source's reduction takes `ℐ' = (g_1, …, g_r)` with `g_i = f^♯(t_i)`, so
`r = s` there; that is not imposed here, because there is no map from the global sections of `𝒪`
to those of `𝒪'` on a general morphism of sites — `f^♯` carries a section over `W` to one over
`f^{-1}W`, which is a compatible family only over the image of `f.u`. What the source's case
needs of the two families is exactly
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible`, the hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 099J],
and that is the input here.

That obstruction is specific to a general morphism of sites. As soon as the target site has a
terminal object whose image is terminal in the source site — which is the case for a morphism of
ringed spaces, and so for a morphism of schemes — the transport exists, `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` is
built from `ℐ` and the relation above is *proved* rather than received:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushGlobalSections` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushforwardIdealCompatible_pushGlobalSections` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionConstructedIdeal.lean`, where each statement below is read at that
pair.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0944](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0944)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099B](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099B)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099D](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099D)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099E](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099E)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099F](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099F)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099J](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099J)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0E](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0E)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0G](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0G)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0I](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0I)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AKL](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AKL)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite TopologicalSpace
open AlgebraicGeometry
open Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedPullbackPushforwardAdjunction

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite

attribute [local instance] HasDerivedCategory.standard
attribute [local instance] AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.hasSheafCompose_forget₂_ringCat

-- [Stacks, Tag 099F] at a sheaf of ideals generated by finitely many global sections is made a
-- local instance, so that `derivedCompletion X (generatedIdealSheaf g)` resolves from the data
-- and no statement below carries an `IsRightAdjoint` hypothesis.
attribute [local instance] isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf

section Site

variable {X Y : RingedSite.{u, u, u}} [HasWeakSheafify X.J CommRingCat.{u}]
  [HasWeakSheafify Y.J CommRingCat.{u}] (f : RingedSite.Hom X Y) {r s : ℕ}
  (g : Fin r → Presheaf.globalSections (X.O.obj ⋙ forget CommRingCat.{u}))
  (t : Fin s → Presheaf.globalSections (Y.O.obj ⋙ forget CommRingCat.{u}))
  (hI : PushforwardIdealCompatible f (generatedIdealSheaf t) (generatedIdealSheaf g))

omit [HasWeakSheafify Y.J CommRingCat.{u}] in
include hI in
/-- **[Stacks, Tag 0A0I], the left hand side of the display, at ideal sheaves generated by
global sections.** `Rf_*(K^∧)` is derived complete along `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s)`, for an arbitrary
`K ∈ D(𝒪')`, the completion being taken along `ℐ' = (g_1, …, g_r)`.

This is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion` with its
instance hypothesis discharged: the derived completion functor on `D(𝒪')` is produced from the
generators by `isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf`, so nothing of
[Stacks, Tag 099F] is assumed. The mathematical content is [Stacks, Tag 099J], that `Rf_*`
preserves derived completeness, applied to the derived-complete object `K^∧`.

What is restricted is the data — the source's `ℐ` is of finite type, hence only locally
generated by finitely many sections — and not the object. -/
theorem isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf
    (K : DerivedCategory (SheafOfModules.{u} ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.O)))) :
    IsDerivedComplete.{u, u, u, u} Y (generatedIdealSheaf t)
      ((derivedPushforward.{u} f.toRingedToposSiteHom).obj
        ((derivedCompletion X (generatedIdealSheaf g)).obj K)) :=
  isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion f _ _ hI K

/-- **The canonical comparison map `(Rf_*K)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)` of [Stacks, Tag 0A0G], at ideal
sheaves generated by global sections.** It exists because `Rf_*(K^∧)` is derived complete, by
[Stacks, Tag 099J], so the universal property of derived completion applies to the natural map
`Rf_*K ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)`; it is built as a mate and is therefore natural in `K`.

This is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison` with both of its
instance hypotheses discharged from the generators, by
`isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf` on either site. The comparison map is
what [Stacks, Tag 0A0G] asserts to be invertible and what the display of [Stacks, Tag 0A0I]
compares its two sides by.

It is invertible at every object of `D(𝒪')` as soon as the two generating families correspond,
`g_i = f^♯(t_i)`:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`, and
`isIso_natTrans_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf` there for the whole
natural transformation. Here the two families are independent and related only by `hI`, which is
all the construction needs. -/
@[stacks 0A0G "the canonical comparison map `(Rf_*K)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)` that the tag asserts to be \
invertible, constructed with nothing assumed, for two **independent** families of global \
sections of the two structure sheaves generating `ℐ` and `ℐ'` and related only by the hypothesis \
of [Stacks, Tag 099J] — the regime in which [Stacks, Tag 099F] is proved in this library, by \
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf`, so that \
both derived completion functors are produced from the data. Its invertibility at every object \
of `D(𝒪')`, which is the tag's assertion, holds as soon as the two families correspond, \
`g_i = f^♯(t_i)`, as the tag's own `ℐ'` does: \
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf` \
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`. At the tag's own `ℐ`, which is of finite type \
and hence only *locally* generated by finitely many sections, the derived completion functors \
come instead from [Stacks, Tag 099F] at a sheaf of ideals of finite type, \
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete`"]
def derivedCompletionPushforwardComparisonGeneratedIdealSheaf :
    derivedPushforward.{u} f.toRingedToposSiteHom ⋙
        derivedCompletion Y (generatedIdealSheaf t) ⟶
      derivedCompletion X (generatedIdealSheaf g) ⋙
        derivedPushforward.{u} f.toRingedToposSiteHom :=
  derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison f _ _ hI

/-- **A criterion for `Rf_*` to commute with derived completion at `K`, with nothing assumed.**
At ideal sheaves generated by finitely many global sections, the comparison map
`(Rf_*K)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)` is invertible as soon as `Rf_*` applied to the completion map
`K ⟶ K^∧` is.

Indeed `Rf_*(K^∧)` is derived complete, so an invertible `Rf_*(K ⟶ K^∧)` makes `Rf_*K` derived
complete too, whence its own completion map is invertible; and the comparison is the remaining
factor.

This is `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isIso_map_unit` with both instance
hypotheses discharged from the generators. By
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_map_derivedCompletionUnit_iff` its hypothesis `hK` holds
exactly when `Rf_*K` is already derived complete, so this is the criterion at those objects. For
an arbitrary `K`, with no condition on `Rf_*K`, the invertibility is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`, once the two generating families correspond. -/
@[stacks 0A0G "the criterion that the comparison map `(Rf_*K)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)` is invertible as \
soon as `Rf_*(K ⟶ K^∧)` is, proved with nothing assumed at two independent families of global \
sections of the two structure sheaves generating `ℐ` and `ℐ'`. This is the reduction the \
source's own proof makes before its local computation, and by \
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_map_derivedCompletionUnit_iff` it covers exactly the objects \
with `Rf_*K` already derived complete. The tag's assertion for an arbitrary object is \
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf` \
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`, which runs the source's computation at two \
corresponding families `g_i = f^♯(t_i)`"]
theorem isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isIso_map_unit
    {K : DerivedCategory (SheafOfModules.{u} ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.O)))}
    (hK : IsIso ((derivedPushforward.{u} f.toRingedToposSiteHom).map
      ((derivedCompletionUnit X (generatedIdealSheaf g)).app K))) :
    IsIso ((derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison f (generatedIdealSheaf t)
      (generatedIdealSheaf g) hI).app K) :=
  isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isIso_map_unit f _ _ hI hK

/-- **[Stacks, Tags 0A0G and 0A0I] at a derived-complete object, with nothing assumed.** At
ideal sheaves generated by finitely many global sections, the comparison map
`(Rf_*K)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(K^∧)` is invertible for every `K ∈ D(𝒪')` that is derived complete along
`ℐ'`, so that `Rf_*(K^∧) = Rf_*K = (Rf_*K)^∧` there.

This is `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isDerivedComplete` with both instance
hypotheses discharged from the generators. What it asks of the object it does not ask of the
data: the two generating families are independent, related only by `hI`. For an arbitrary object
the two families have to correspond, `g_i = f^♯(t_i)`, and then the display holds by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`. -/
@[stacks 0A0G "the display `Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧` for a **derived-complete** `K`, proved with \
nothing assumed at two **independent** families of global sections of the two structure sheaves \
generating `ℐ` and `ℐ'` and related only by the hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 099J] — the regime in \
which [Stacks, Tag 099F] is proved here, so that both derived completion functors are produced \
from those generators. For an **arbitrary** `K` the display holds once the two families \
correspond, `g_i = f^♯(t_i)`, as the source's own `ℐ'` does: \
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf` \
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`"]
theorem isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete
    {K : DerivedCategory (SheafOfModules.{u} ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.O)))}
    (hK : IsDerivedComplete.{u, u, u, u} X (generatedIdealSheaf g) K) :
    IsIso ((derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison f (generatedIdealSheaf t)
      (generatedIdealSheaf g) hI).app K) :=
  isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isDerivedComplete f _ _ hI hK

end Site

end AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite

namespace Stacks.RingedSpace

attribute [local instance] HasDerivedCategory.standard
attribute [local instance] AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.hasSheafCompose_forget₂_ringCat

open AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite (generatedIdealSheaf)

/-- **[Stacks, Tag 099F] on the small Zariski site of a ringed space, at a sheaf of ideals
generated by finitely many global sections**: the inclusion of the objects of `D(𝒪_X)` derived
complete along `(g_1, …, g_r)` has a left adjoint. This is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf` with the
sheafification hypothesis of the small Zariski site discharged.

It is stated separately, and made a local instance below, only so that instance search has a
hypothesis-free declaration to match: searching for the ringed-site instance on
`Stacks.RingedSpace.toRingedSite X` leaves `HasWeakSheafify (toRingedSite X).J CommRingCat` as a
subgoal, which search does not solve even though `inferInstance` does. -/
theorem isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf
    (X : AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.{u, u}) {r : ℕ}
    (g : Fin r → CategoryTheory.Presheaf.globalSections
      ((toRingedSite X).O.obj ⋙ forget CommRingCat.{u})) :
    (ObjectProperty.ι (RingedSite.IsDerivedComplete.{u, u, u, u} (toRingedSite X)
      (generatedIdealSheaf g))).IsRightAdjoint :=
  RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf _ g

attribute [local instance] isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf

section Display

variable {X Y : AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.{u, u}} (f : X ⟶ Y) {r s : ℕ}
  (g : Fin r → CategoryTheory.Presheaf.globalSections
    ((toRingedSite X).O.obj ⋙ forget CommRingCat.{u}))
  (t : Fin s → CategoryTheory.Presheaf.globalSections
    ((toRingedSite Y).O.obj ⋙ forget CommRingCat.{u}))
  (hI : RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible (toRingedSiteHom f)
    (generatedIdealSheaf t) (generatedIdealSheaf g))

include hI in
/-- **[Stacks, Tag 0A0I], the left hand side of the display, at a morphism of ringed spaces and
ideal sheaves generated by global sections.** `Rf_*(ℱ^∧)` is derived complete along
`ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s)`, for an arbitrary `ℱ ∈ D(𝒪_X)`.

This is the ringed-site statement
`RingedSite.isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf` read at
the small Zariski sites of `X` and `Y`. Nothing is assumed: the derived completion functor on
`D(𝒪_X)` is produced from the generators of `ℐ'`. -/
theorem isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf
    (K : DerivedCategory (_root_.SheafOfModules.{u}
      ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (toRingedSite X).O)))) :
    RingedSite.IsDerivedComplete.{u, u, u, u} (toRingedSite Y) (generatedIdealSheaf t)
      ((derivedPushforward.{u} (toRingedSiteHom f).toRingedToposSiteHom).obj
        ((RingedSite.derivedCompletion (toRingedSite X) (generatedIdealSheaf g)).obj K)) :=
  RingedSite.isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf
    (toRingedSiteHom f) g t hI K

/-- **[Stacks, Tag 0A0I] at a morphism of ringed spaces, for a derived-complete object, with
nothing assumed.** For ideal sheaves generated by finitely many global sections, the comparison
`(Rf_*ℱ)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(ℱ^∧)` is invertible whenever `ℱ` is derived complete along `ℐ'`.

This is
`RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete`
read at the small Zariski sites; both derived completion functors are produced from the
generators, so no part of [Stacks, Tag 099F] is a hypothesis. The restriction to a
derived-complete `ℱ` is the tag's own content and remains; the invertibility for an arbitrary
`ℱ` is the assertion of [Stacks, Tag 0A0G].

The same statement at the source's own data, two sheaves of ideals of finite type, is
`isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isFiniteType_of_isDerivedComplete` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`; this one is the case of it in which the sheaves
of ideals are generated by global sections, by
`Stacks.RingedSpace.isFiniteType_generatedIdealSheaf` there. -/
theorem isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete
    {K : DerivedCategory (_root_.SheafOfModules.{u}
      ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (toRingedSite X).O)))}
    (hK : RingedSite.IsDerivedComplete.{u, u, u, u} (toRingedSite X)
      (generatedIdealSheaf g) K) :
    IsIso ((RingedSite.derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison (toRingedSiteHom f)
      (generatedIdealSheaf t) (generatedIdealSheaf g) hI).app K) :=
  RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete
    (toRingedSiteHom f) g t hI hK

end Display

end Stacks.RingedSpace

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme

attribute [local instance] HasDerivedCategory.standard
attribute [local instance] AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.hasSheafCompose_forget₂_ringCat
attribute [local instance]
  Stacks.RingedSpace.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf

open AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite (generatedIdealSheaf)

section Display

variable {X S : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ S) {r s : ℕ}
  (g : Fin r → CategoryTheory.Presheaf.globalSections
    ((Stacks.RingedSpace.toRingedSite X.toRingedSpace).O.obj ⋙
      CategoryTheory.forget CommRingCat.{u}))
  (t : Fin s → CategoryTheory.Presheaf.globalSections
    ((Stacks.RingedSpace.toRingedSite S.toRingedSpace).O.obj ⋙
      CategoryTheory.forget CommRingCat.{u}))
  (hI : RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible (toRingedSiteHom f)
    (generatedIdealSheaf t) (generatedIdealSheaf g))

/-- **[Stacks, Tag 0A0I] in the display's own setting, for a derived-complete object, with
nothing assumed.** Let `f : X ⟶ S` be a morphism of schemes, let `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s) ⊆ 𝒪_S` and
`ℐ' = (g_1, …, g_r) ⊆ 𝒪_X` be the sheaves of ideals generated by finitely many global sections
of the two structure sheaves, with `f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ) ⊆ ℐ'` — which the display's `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`
satisfies. Then `(Rf_*ℱ)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(ℱ^∧)` is invertible for every `ℱ ∈ D(𝒪_X)` derived complete
along `ℐ'`.

Both derived completion functors are produced from the generators, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf`, so
[Stacks, Tag 099F] is nowhere a hypothesis; neither quasi-coherence nor finite type nor
quasi-coherence of `ℱ` is assumed either, those entering the source only through that tag. The
restriction to a derived-complete `ℱ` is the tag's own content and remains; the invertibility
for an arbitrary `ℱ` is the assertion of [Stacks, Tag 0A0G]. The descent from a globally
generated `ℐ` to a finite-type one is carried out in
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`, where the derived completion functors come from
[Stacks, Tag 099F] at a sheaf of ideals of finite type; this statement is the case of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme`
`.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isFiniteType_of_isDerivedComplete` there in
which the two sheaves of ideals are generated by global sections.

Because `ℱ` ranges over the derived-complete objects of `D(𝒪_X)` and not over quasi-coherent
sheaves alone, this is also the analogue of the display *for complexes of quasi-coherent
modules* that the fifth and last sentence of [Stacks, Tag 0AKL] attributes to
[HL-P, Theorem 6.5]; related statements are
`isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isFiniteType_of_isDerivedComplete` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`, the same statement at two sheaves of ideals of
finite type, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_forall_isNilpotent` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionSpaces.lean`, which takes an arbitrary object in the nilpotent
regime and whose module documentation accounts for the remark sentence by sentence. -/
